Transactional in-app event publication
======================================

The notification bell row and its pending realtime event are flushed together.
Shared notification dispatch leaves commit/rollback to the business owner. A
standalone create_in_app call may retain commit=True. No realtime event is
published inside that creation call, including when commit=False is used.

The worker polls committed pending rows every five seconds on the default
queue. Each row is locked with SKIP LOCKED through publication and commit, so
competing workers do not intentionally handle the same row simultaneously.
The worker rechecks the current notification, recipient, organization, active
and deleted state, and read/dismissed state. It suppresses ineligible rows.
The captured tenant and recipient cannot be replaced by a later reassignment;
hard deletion cascades pending work instead of promoting it to platform scope.
A platform-originated personal notification captures the recipient's actual
organization at creation. Organizationless platform users retain their bell
rows; the existing event WebSocket rejects organizationless broadcasts.

A failed/uncertain publish or zero subscribers leaves the event pending with
backoff capped at60seconds. Each invocation handles at most50rows by default,
with a20second loop budget and a3second individual publication timeout. A
Redis/Valkey acknowledgment with subscribers means publication was accepted;
it is not an acknowledgment from a browser, another handler or an end user.
The persisted bell remains available through the normal notifications API.

A crash after publication but before database commit can cause a duplicate.
Retries use the same event ID, correlation ID, timestamp and content for an
unchanged notification. This is not exactly-once delivery. There is no claim
that arbitrary downstream handlers deduplicate these events or that a change
made after the final eligibility check is fenced against publication.

Deployment and migration
------------------------
Apply020_inapp_event_outbox before running new API/worker code. Roll out the
matching API, workers and beat together: an old API still publishes directly,
and an old worker does not register the new task. Pending rows remain durable
while workers or the broker are unavailable, provided PostgreSQL is retained.

Fresh installs and upgrades from the frozen legacy schema are checked. Existing
bell rows are preserved without inventing or replaying historical events. A
downgrade refuses pending events; an operator must reconcile them explicitly.
No automatic marking, discarding or truncation is provided by the migration.
Completed/suppressed outbox rows remain until their bell, user or organization
is physically deleted; an independent retention policy remains operational work.

The camera-ingest, plugin-alert and remote-scan owners that already commit their
source record before notifying now commit the separate notification transaction
explicitly. A notification failure cannot undo the already-persisted source
record. A crash between those two transactions can still omit the notification;
this change does not introduce a durable job for every source event.

Qualification and limits
------------------------
Behavioral checks reproduce the old early publication/owner-commit defects.
Independent PostgreSQL sessions check visibility, rollback, row locks, concurrent
workers, current authority and migration safety. Additional isolated fixtures use
actual Redis/Valkey and separate task processes, including SIGKILL before commit
and after acknowledged publication while the database write is blocked. The
latter demonstrates recovery with duplicate publication rather than concealing
it. These fixtures do not exercise customer hardware or send external messages.

External email/SMS/webhook delivery and generic retry scheduling are unchanged:
their database/queue commit gap and ambiguous external acceptance remain open.
This in-app outbox is not the existing SLA report SMTP outbox and does not reuse
its approval, delivery-epoch or report recovery semantics. It is not a general
application event outbox or a complete disaster-recovery/retention qualification.
